# Task 02: layered Nangate45 measurement, ONE partition per yosys invocation.
# scripts/remote_yosys_round.sh substitutes @PART_TAG@/@PART_TOP@/@PART_CHPARAM@/
# @PART_BLACKBOX@/@READFILES@/LIB and runs each partition under its own
# timeout, so a slow/heavy module cannot discard the per-module area/timing
# already measured for the others (Round-09's single flattened top-level ABC
# stalled at post-flatten OPT_MERGE on 722,367 cells and never reached ABC).
#
# Round-18 (synthesis-usability repair): the previous single `abc` call mapped
# every module of the partition in one pass, starting with the partition top.
# When the top is a giant leaf (Round-04 evidence: sigmoid_from_q14_mod grew to
# 419,612 gate cells after techmap, sigmoid_pwl >300 s in ABC), that one ABC
# run stalled inside "57.2 Extracting gate netlist of module ..." and the
# partition timed out BEFORE any "Chip area for module" line was emitted, so
# every partition containing the giant -- and every partition queued behind it
# -- reported no area/timing at all.  The flow below therefore keeps hierarchy
# and maps per-leaf-module with a bounded window each:
#
#   pass 1 (leaves first): `select @PART_TOP@ %n` selects everything EXCEPT the
#     partition top (a bare module pattern followed by the %n complement; no
#     -add/-delete, so it works across Yosys versions).  `abc` maps each leaf
#     independently and `stat -liberty` emits their area lines immediately, so
#     a giant top can no longer discard the leaves' results.
#   pass 2 (top last): `select @PART_TOP@` selects the top alone and maps it in
#     its own bounded window.  Its submodule instances are already-mapped
#     blackboxes, so this reports the top's own gates.  Even when this window
#     times out, the leaves' area/timing and the pre-ABC per-module cell counts
#     are already on record.
#
# Area is emitted exactly once per module: the LEAF_AREA_DONE stat covers the
# non-top modules and the TOP_AREA_DONE stat covers the top, so summing the
# "Chip area for module" lines inside a partition's log gives the partition's
# standard-cell logic area without double-counting a leaf that also appears in
# the top's subtree.
#
# Hierarchy is PRESERVED (no top-level `flatten`).  An early `stat` right
# after `proc`/`fsm` lowering and BEFORE `memory -nomap` reports the $mem
# bits as SRAM/ROM capacity; the per-leaf and final `stat -liberty` report
# mapped logic area only ($mem_v2 bits are not counted by `stat` in Yosys
# 0.64), so each partition's memory bits are emitted exactly once.
#
# Every number below is a pre-layout per-module estimate (partition-level,
# leaves mapped first then the top; data-plane leaves blackboxed in the
# control-plane closure) -- NOT a full no-blackbox top-level closure, so
# measurement_scope stays PARTIAL_HIERARCHICAL.
#
# Round-19 (measurement completion): the ABC invocations below switch to the
# lightweight single-round `-fast` script (previously `-script
# +strash;map;topo;stime,-c` appended to ABC's multi-pass default).  The
# Nangate45 liberty, `-D 10000` (10 ns) target and the input/output delay
# constraint file are UNCHANGED, so every number remains a Nangate45 pre-layout
# estimate; the lighter script only trades mapping effort for runtime so a
# partition is likelier to reach `stat -liberty` inside its bounded window.
# This calibre difference is a script diff (recorded in the report), not a
# library change.  Each `stat` checkpoint is additionally mirrored with `tee`
# to build/ppa_measure.<tag>.<stage>.stat so area/timing evidence survives even
# if the harness's yosys.log tail is truncated on a timeout.
#
# Round-24 (measurement completion, scheduler fix): the per-module two-pass
# flow below is UNCHANGED this round -- it remains the measure contract (leaves
# mapped first in pass 1, top mapped last in pass 2, hierarchy kept, one
# partition per yosys invocation, Nangate45 liberty / `-D 10000` (10 ns) target
# / input-output delay constraint unchanged).  The operative change is the
# partition schedule in harness/ppa.toml: cheap known-real leaves lead so a
# short probe lands real area/timing first, the unmeasured q4-matvec leaves
# follow so the probe's log frontier records which partition is heaviest, and
# the memory-bearing closures stay reachable when budget allows.  No functional
# change to the measurement calibre; every number remains a pre-layout
# per-module estimate with measurement_scope=PARTIAL_HIERARCHICAL.
#
# Round-25 (measurement completion, hierarchical split): ppa.toml `blackbox`
# is now a space-separated MODULE-NAME LIST.  scripts/remote_yosys_round.sh
# filters harness/ppa_leaves_blackbox.v down to exactly those names into
# build/ppa_bb_<name>.v and injects `read_verilog -sv -lib build/ppa_bb_<name>.v`
# via @PART_BLACKBOX@, so a partition's OWN top (never in the list) stays real
# while its heavy q_bus x x_bus MAC subtree is abstracted.  The enclosing
# q4-matvec partitions then complete their own glue area instead of timing out
# in one 37x24 MAC techmap/ABC column; the real MAC area is measured by the
# standalone q4_dequant_matvec_ch partition.  The PARTITION_BLACKBOX log line
# below records which modules were abstracted, so a wrapper-only area row is
# never mistaken for the subtree's full area.
#
# Round-31 (measurement completion, end-to-end convergence): l2norm_q is added
# to the KDA closure's blackbox list (kda_recurrence_contract, alongside
# kda_delta_col; cp_fsm already blackboxed it).  `select <top> %n` and abc skip
# blackboxed modules automatically, so the closure maps its wrapper glue inside
# a bounded window and its $mem accounting reaches MEMORY_STAT_COMPLETE instead
# of stalling on l2norm_q's ~886k-cell pre-ABC body (isqrt64 32-stage + 64-bit
# $div + 32 64x24 multiplies).  The standalone `l2norm_q` leaf partition
# (top=l2norm_q, blackbox="") measures that REAL body and is the leaf-wrapper
# reference: its stat -liberty area and ABC wire-load delay are the true
# l2norm_q baseline, and the closure's blackbox-boundary timing (ABC cannot
# propagate delay through a -lib box) is cross-checked against this leaf result
# rather than read as a chip critical path.
#
# Round-36 (measurement completion, harness fix): the harness no longer writes
# build/ppa_measure.constr and the abc invocations below no longer pass
# `-constr`.  This ABC build's read_constr rejected the harness-written
# set_input_delay/set_output_delay file ("Unrecognized token"), so the 2000 ps
# boundary margin was silently dead.  Removing it makes the calibre explicit:
# every delay below is a raw pre-layout combinational path estimate with NO
# input/output boundary margin and NO STA -- exactly as measured, never
# confused with a constrained path.
# Round-31 note (kept verbatim for history) follows.
# The `-constr
# build/ppa_measure.constr` I/O delay applies at the partition boundary; this
# ABC build's read_constr rejects the harness-written set_input_delay/
# set_output_delay file (non-fatal, logged as "Unrecognized token"), so that
# 2000 ps margin is currently NOT applied -- every delay below remains a raw
# pre-layout combinational path estimate, NOT STA.
#
# 2026-09-10 (Task-02 measurement completion, end-to-end convergence; upper-Pro
# batch directive -- the ONLY change of this round): every `abc` invocation
# below now runs the library-driven fanout-repair pass `buffer` on the MAPPED
# netlist, between `topo` and `stime`.  The edit is inside the `-script` string
# only; the `abc` options themselves (`-liberty`, `-D 10000` = 10 ns, `-dff`)
# and the read order are untouched.
#
# Why: the top_level_sta closure STA reported the single arc
# `writer/_37622_/ZN` (INV_X1) at 1,482,919 ps and left
# `sta_artifact_fanout_status=unresolved`.  That arc is an out-of-table
# extrapolation of one net that fans out to roughly eight thousand gate inputs
# -- a mapping artifact of a flow that never buffers, not logic depth.
# `abc -liberty` maps without any fanout repair, so nothing bounded that net;
# `buffer` inserts the library's own buffer cells until every non-primary-input
# node drives at most `-N` loads (default `nDegree` = ten, and with the default
# `fAddBufs` it uses real buffers rather than inverter pairs) and re-sizes gates
# against the same Liberty timing -- exactly the fanout repair a pre-layout flow
# needs before STA.  It adds real cells from the mapped technology library
# (buffer cells plus whatever the sizing changes) and changes no module port, no
# primary input/output and no functional behaviour; the existing instances keep
# their connectivity except that a high-fanout driver net is re-driven through
# the inserted buffer cells.
#
# Placement is deliberate: after `map` (so the preceding `strash;map` cannot
# swallow the tree), before `stime` (so the logged `Delay = ... ps` line
# describes the buffered netlist) and before the `clean`/`stat -liberty`
# checkpoints (so the added cells are counted in the area rows and show up in
# the cell histogram).  The ABC log echoes `ABC: + buffer` immediately before
# `ABC: + stime -c`; that per-partition echo is the attribution that the pass
# really ran, and a partition without it has no buffering evidence.
#
# Calibre is otherwise unchanged: same Nangate45 typical library, same read
# order, same 25-module blackbox list, same per-module pre-layout measurement
# scope (PARTIAL_HIERARCHICAL for every partition that keeps a blackbox).  The
# expected visible effect is that standard-cell logic area grows (buffers and
# the sizing are real cells) while the reported wire-load delay falls; both are
# reported exactly as measured, and neither is a full-chip Fmax.
#
# 2026-09-10 (Task-02 flop handover; upper-Pro batch directive -- the ONLY
# change of this round): all four `abc` invocations below now run with the
# partition's flops STILL IN THEIR INTERNAL FORM (`$_DFF_P_` / `$_DFF_PN0_`
# from `techmap`), and each `dfflibmap -liberty LIB` line moves from BEFORE its
# `abc` to immediately AFTER it.
#
# Why: the previous round's `buffer` pass removed the combinational high-fanout
# artifact (`writer/_37622_/ZN` went from 1,482,919 ps to gone), but the
# remaining worst arc became a TIMING-CELL arc -- `writer/_78655_/Q` (DFFR_X1)
# at 32,983 ps -- with `sta_artifact_fanout_status=unresolved`.  Root cause:
# the flow ran `dfflibmap` BEFORE `abc`, so by the time ABC started there were
# no `$dff`/`$_DFF_*` cells left (the log shows `mapped 68461 $_DFF_PN0_ cells
# to \DFFR_X1` / `mapped 167424 $_DFF_P_ cells to \DFF_X1` happening first).
# A flop Q net is then, from ABC's point of view, a net driven by an opaque
# library box -- indistinguishable from a primary input -- so `buffer` could
# only repair nets inside the combinational cone and never the Q nets that
# actually fan out to thousands of gate inputs.  Handing the flops to ABC
# (`abc ... -dff`) puts those Q drivers inside ABC's network, where the same
# `buffer` pass can build the library buffer tree on them; the following
# `dfflibmap` then maps the returned `$_DFF_*` cells through the same
# Nangate45 typical Liberty.
#
# Scope of the edit: the `dfflibmap -liberty LIB` line is MOVED, not changed --
# the same Liberty, the same `abc -liberty LIB -D 10000 ... -dff` options, the
# same read order, the same blackbox list, the same `clean` / `stat -liberty` /
# MEMORY_STAT_COMPLETE checkpoints.  A side benefit is that each pass's
# pre-ABC `stat` now lands AFTER techmap and BEFORE abc, so it prints the
# unmapped `$_DFF_PN0_`/`$_DFF_P_` counts -- the attribution that the flops
# really were handed to ABC (`DFFR_X1`/`DFF_X1` must reappear in the
# post-`dfflibmap` `stat -liberty` rows).  No module port, no primary
# input/output and no functional behaviour changes; the only netlist delta is
# added BUF/INV cells and their area.  Calibre is otherwise unchanged, and
# every number stays a pre-layout per-module estimate (PARTIAL_HIERARCHICAL).
#
# 2026-09-10 (Task-02 fanout-split completeness; upper-Pro batch directive --
# the ONLY change of this round): the four `abc` invocations below now pass
# `-constr build/ppa_measure.constr` (the file is written once per partition by
# the `tee -o ... log "set_driving_cell INV_X1"` line further down, immediately
# before the closure block), and their `buffer` step now carries `-p`.
# Nothing else in those lines changed: same `-liberty LIB`, same `-D 10000`
# (10 ns), same `-dff`, same script order (`strash;map;topo;buffer;stime -c`).
#
# Why `-constr`: ABC's library-driven `buffer` pass only repairs fanout on nets
# it is willing to re-drive, and the sizing-based implementation this build runs
# (`Scl_CommandBuffer` -> `Abc_SclBufferingPerform` -> `Abc_SclBufSize`) admits a
# CI driver ONLY when a PI driving cell is declared:
# `(Abc_ObjIsNode(pObj) && Abc_ObjFaninNum(pObj) > 0) || (Abc_ObjIsCi(pObj) &&
# p->pPiDrive)`.  `Abc_ObjIsCi` is `ABC_OBJ_PI || ABC_OBJ_BO`, and a flop Q net
# is a BO (Yosys hands the partition's `$_DFF_P_`/`$_DFF_PN0_` cells to ABC as
# latches with box terminals, which is the `-dff` form ABC sees after the
# `dfflibmap` move).  `pPiDrive` is set from `Abc_FrameReadDrivingCell()`, which
# only the constr line `set_driving_cell <cell>` populates (read back by
# `Scl_CommandReadConstr` -> `Abc_SclReadTimingConstr`).  Without a constr the
# pass repaired internal nodes -- `logit_writer` already carries BUF_X4/X8/X16 --
# yet never a flop Q net, which is the measured defect: OpenSTA extrapolated the
# CK->Q table of `writer/_46333_/Q` to 33,985 ps and left
# `sta_artifact_fanout_status=unresolved`, and the earlier `buffer` round only
# moved the worst arc from a combinational pin onto that flop pin.  `buffer -p`
# is passed as well because it is the documented "buffering primary inputs"
# toggle of the same command: the CI gate that matters here is `pPiDrive`, but in
# an ABC build whose `buffer` routes to the legacy `Abc_SclBufPerform` the same
# intent is expressed by `fBufPis`, so the flag keeps this round's hypothesis
# testable on either implementation instead of silently doing nothing.
design -reset
@PART_BLACKBOX@
read_verilog -sv -defer @READFILES@
hierarchy -check -top @PART_TOP@ @PART_CHPARAM@
log "=== PARTITION @PART_TAG@: hierarchy done (hierarchy kept, no flatten) ==="
log "PARTITION_BLACKBOX=@PART_BLACKBOX_LIST@"
proc; opt -fast; fsm; opt -fast
# Early stat BEFORE `memory -nomap`: Yosys `stat` reports "Number of memories" /
# "Number of memory bits" only for $mem cells.  `memory -nomap` still runs
# memory_collect (it only skips the dff/share/map/memx/bram/libmap/macc stages),
# folding $mem into $mem_v2, whose bits Yosys 0.64 `stat` no longer counts
# (round-04 log shows "2 $mem_v2" with a "-" memory-bits row), which is why
# sram_total_bits was 未测得.  This line emits the $mem bits exactly once per
# partition; the post-collect `stat -liberty` below still reports mapped area
# without re-emitting bits, so the controller's cross-partition sum is not
# doubled.
stat
log "MEMORY_STAT_COMPLETE=@PART_TAG@"
memory -nomap; opt -fast
log "=== PARTITION @PART_TAG@: proc/fsm/memory lowering done ==="
# Round-40 (measurement completion, end-to-end convergence): complete the
# memory collection and run the combinational-loop diagnostics BEFORE any
# techmap/ABC.  Round-39's log showed the standalone MEMORY_COLLECT only at
# pass 65 -- after every partition's ABC had already timed the netlist.  This
# explicit `memory_collect` finalizes the $mem_v2 form up front (the $mem bits
# were already emitted by the early stat above, MEMORY_STAT_COMPLETE), so
# every per-module ABC wire-load delay in this partition is measured on a
# fully-collected netlist.  `check` prints "Found a combinational loop"
# for every loop in the partition -- a loop, not wire-load, is what makes a
# delay non-physical -- so the tee'd pre_abc.check log separates "RTL comb
# loop" from "acyclic deep comb chain (wire-load artifact)" and records the
# artifact-partition evidence per partition.
# Round-42 (measurement completion, harness fix): the Round-40 diagnostics
# were written as TWO lines -- a bare `tee -o <file>` (a no-op with no
# command, opening the file empty) followed by a plain `check` on the main
# log -- so the verdict never reached the check file and plain `check` did not
# force loop detection.
# Round-43 (measurement completion, harness fix): this container's Yosys build
# rejects `check -loop` ("Command syntax error: Unknown option or option in
# arguments", seen on every non-macro partition), so the single-line tee+check
# uses bare `check`.  The tee'd pre_abc.check file still captures each
# partition's acyclic / "Found a combinational loop" verdict, and loop
# classification evidence is read from that file -- a loop, not wire-load, is
# what makes a delay non-physical.  `-loop`-forced detection is NOT available
# in this build; verdicts rely on plain `check` output.
memory_collect
log "MEMORY_PRE_ABC_COLLECT_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.pre_abc.check check
log "LOOP_CHECK_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# Round-40: SRAM/ROM macro blackbox declaration.  mla_kv_cache is the only
# memory-bearing SUBMODULE in the golden closure (cp_fsm instantiates it; the
# PWL/LUT memories live in partition tops and stay real).  Removing its body
# before techmap keeps its memory read-port logic out of ABC's wire-load
# delays; its 245,760 bits were already counted by the early stat
# (MEMORY_STAT_COMPLETE) and are accounted by the ~1 Mbit/mm2 macro model.
# The `m:*` module selection is a no-op in every other partition.
blackbox m:*mla_kv_cache*
log "SRAM_MACRO_BLACKBOX_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# Round-35 (measurement completion): `@PART_MACRO@` is replaced by
# scripts/remote_yosys_round.sh with an empty string for normal partitions.
# Partitions declared `macro_blackbox = true` in harness/ppa.toml (currently
# kda_recurrence_contract and q4_embedding_lookup) NO LONGER use this template:
# the harness routes them to the standalone harness/ppa_macro.ys, which reads
# the real RTL body, emits the early `stat` $mem bits (memory-stat stays
# counted via MEMORY_STAT_COMPLETE), logs the macro markers, then reaches EOF
# without any techmap/ABC and without an `exit` command -- Round-34's injected
# `exit 0` returned rc=1 because this container's Yosys has no `exit` command.
# Stopping before techmap/ABC keeps the flattened 9.2-Mbit memory from mapping
# to ~792k gates / 26 us wire-load and destroying the 10 ns comparison.  Their
# area is accounted by the macro model (~1 Mbit/mm2), not as standard cells.
@PART_MACRO@
# 2026-09-10 (Task-02 fanout-split completeness; upper-Pro batch directive --
# the ONLY source change of this round): declare ABC's PI driving cell so the
# SCL `buffer` pass actually splits the flop-Q (CI) nets.  Root cause from the
# ABC source, not from a guess: `Abc_BufManAddNode` refused CI drivers
# ("allow PIs to have high fanout"), and the sizing-based pass this build uses
# (`Scl_CommandBuffer` -> `Abc_SclBufferingPerform` -> `Abc_SclBufSize`) filters
# with `(Abc_ObjIsNode(pObj) && Abc_ObjFaninNum(pObj) > 0) || (Abc_ObjIsCi(pObj)
# && p->pPiDrive)`.  `Abc_ObjIsCi` is `ABC_OBJ_PI || ABC_OBJ_BO`, i.e. PIs AND
# latch/box outputs, and every flop Q net is a BO -- but `pPiDrive` stays NULL
# unless `Abc_FrameReadDrivingCell()` is set, which only `set_driving_cell` in
# the constr file ABC reads via Yosys's `abc -constr` can do.  With no constr
# the pass repaired every internal node (hence BUF_X4/X8/X16 already in the
# netlist) yet never a flop Q net, which is exactly the measured defect:
# STA extrapolated the CK->Q table of `writer/_46333_/Q` to 33,985 ps.
# `tee -o` truncates-or-creates `build/ppa_measure.constr` inside each
# partition run, so this fix needs no second harness file; ABC's read_constr
# then prints `Setting driving cell to be "INV_X1".` as the per-partition
# attribution.  The `log` argument is passed WITHOUT quotes: with the quotes the
# log echo wrote the quote characters into the file (ABC then reported
# `Unrecognized token ""set_driving_cell".` and never set a driving cell -- see
# the note right below).
# INV_X1 is the weakest Nangate45 driver and is present in the typical Liberty
# the flow already reads; if the name were absent ABC prints
# `Cannot find the default PI driving cell ...` and this round's hypothesis is
# falsified instead of silently untested.  No RTL, no Liberty, no `-D 10000`,
# no `-dff`, no blackbox list, no read order and no checkpoint changed.
tee -o build/ppa_measure.constr log set_driving_cell INV_X1
# 2026-09-10 (Task-02 flop-Q PI handover; upper-Pro batch directive -- the ONLY
# source change of this round, superseding the `-dff` / `dfflibmap`-order notes
# above): every `abc` invocation in both flow blocks below no longer passes
# `-dff`, and each `dfflibmap -liberty LIB` moves back from AFTER its `abc` to
# BEFORE it -- the standard Yosys QoR order (`dfflibmap`, then `abc`).
#
# Why: last round's `buffer` pass ran with the flops still in their internal
# `$_DFF_PN0_`/`$_DFF_P_` form (`abc -dff`), where a flop Q net reaches ABC as a
# BO (box/latch output); the SCL `buffer` pass this build runs admits a CI
# driver only through `(Abc_ObjIsNode(pObj) && Abc_ObjFaninNum(pObj) > 0) ||
# (Abc_ObjIsCi(pObj) && p->pPiDrive)`, and `p->pPiDrive` is populated only by
# the constr's `set_driving_cell` line.  Last round that line never landed: the
# quoted `log "..."` argument was echoed into `build/ppa_measure.constr` WITH
# its quote characters, so ABC logged `Unrecognized token ""set_driving_cell".`
# and no `Setting driving cell to be "INV_X1".` appears anywhere in that run's
# log.  Measured defect (runs/02_yosys_ppa--20260910-224144--31805): STA still
# extrapolated the CK->Q table of `writer/_46333_/Q` to 33,985 ps -- 93% of the
# 36.532 ns arrival, slack -28.532 ns -- and `sta_artifact_fanout_status` stayed
# `unresolved`.
#
# This round therefore hands ABC a netlist whose flops are already mapped to the
# same Liberty's `DFF_X1`/`DFFR_X1` cells (Q nets on the CI/PI side) AND writes
# the constr line without quotes, so `set_driving_cell INV_X1` is actually
# parsed.  Both halves are one atomic harness change.  Falsifiable criterion, on
# the closure smoke's `report_checks` worst path: the single 33.985 ns
# `writer/_46333_/Q` arc is replaced by a multi-level buffer chain (that pin's
# arc < 1 ns) and the worst slack narrows clearly => the mechanism holds; a
# remaining single flop-Q arc > 25 ns => falsified (next lever: a second
# `buffer` after `abc`, or `insbuf` after `dfflibmap`).
#
# Scope of the edit: the `dfflibmap -liberty LIB` line is MOVED, the `abc`
# options are otherwise untouched (`-liberty LIB`, `-D 10000`, the same
# `-constr build/ppa_measure.constr`, the same
# `-script +strash;map;topo;buffer,-p;stime,-c`), and the constr keeps the same
# cell.  No RTL, no Liberty, no blackbox list, no read order, no
# `clean`/`stat -liberty`/`*.stat` checkpoint, no MEMORY_STAT_COMPLETE and no
# CLOSURE_*_ABC_DONE marker changed.  Every flop is still mapped through the same
# Nangate45 typical Liberty, so no module port, no primary input/output and no
# functional behaviour changes; the netlist delta is the flop-mapping order plus
# whatever real library buffer cells `buffer` inserts on Q nets (reported as
# measured, not predicted).  Every number stays a pre-layout estimate and
# `measurement_scope` stays PARTIAL_HIERARCHICAL -- the closure still blackboxes
# the compute leaves, so this is never a full-chip Fmax.
# 2026-09-11 (Task-02 measurement completion; upper-Pro batch directive -- the
# ONLY change of this round): the four `abc` invocations below drop the `-c`
# flag from their `-script ...;stime,-c` tail (now plain `stime`).  Nothing else
# in those lines changed: same `-liberty LIB`, same `-D 10000` (10 ns), same
# `-constr build/ppa_measure.constr`, same `strash;map;topo;buffer,-p;stime`
# order.
#
# Why `-c` is a measurement artifact, not a critical path: in this ABC build
# `-c` is NOT "print the critical path" -- `Scl_CommandStime` does
# `case 'c': fUseWireLoads ^= 1;` ("toggle using wire-loads [default = no]"),
# and `Abc_SclManStart` attaches the Liberty `default_wire_load` to the
# wire-load model ONLY when `fUseWireLoads` is set.  The Nangate45 typical
# Liberty defaults to `5K_hvratio_1_1`, so with `-c` every stime runs under that
# 5K fanout model, whose per-net capacitance `Abc_SclFindWireCaps` extrapolates
# on the huge-fanout nets of a ~851k-gate partition.  The reported
# `nano_golden_datapath_fsm` `WireLoad="5K_hvratio_1_1" Delay=219114.81 ps`
# (check=acyclic) is therefore that model's extrapolation, not logic depth: the
# SAME closed netlist's OpenSTA in-table worst path is 2.000 ns, ~110x smaller.
# `Abc_SclTimeNtkPrint` prints its `WireLoad = "%s" ... Area ... Delay = N ps`
# line unconditionally, so with no wire-load model selected the log shows
# `WireLoad = "none"` and the harness's `WireLoad|Delay =` grep, seq_pct
# classification and delay_artifact_count calibre are all unchanged.
#
# Falsifiable criterion (closure smoke's ABC line): the FSM `Delay` collapses
# to the ~2-10 ns order => artifact confirmed; still > 100 ns => hypothesis
# falsified and the next lever is `stime -p` per-stage path printing.  Zero RTL
# change; same Nangate45 library, same 25-module blackbox list, same read order;
# every number stays a pre-layout per-module estimate and `measurement_scope`
# stays PARTIAL_HIERARCHICAL (the compute leaves are still blackboxed -- this is
# never a full-chip Fmax).
# ===CLOSURE_FLOW_BEGIN===
# Round-46 (measurement completion, end-to-end convergence): top-level closure
# STA path.  Runs ONLY for the top_level_sta partition
# (top=nano_golden_decoder_top); scripts/remote_yosys_round.sh deletes this
# block for every ordinary partition.
#
# Round-60 (harness repair): the closure netlist MUST be self-contained so the
# single-netlist `top_level_sta` smoke links without the cp_* control-plane
# partitions.  Pre-Round-60 this flow mapped ONLY the top's own glue and wrote
# build/ppa_closure_top.v alone; the top's five control-plane submodules
# (mem_arbiter4, nano_golden_datapath_fsm, image_header_reader, logit_writer,
# nano_golden_microop_controller) were referenced by the top but their mapped
# bodies were absent from the assembled STA netlist, so link_design resolved
# the first of them as a missing LEF macro -> ORD-2013 "instance arb LEF master
# mem_arbiter4 not found".  The flow below maps the WHOLE non-blackbox
# control-plane closure (leaves first, top last -- the same proven two-pass
# pattern as the NORMAL flow) and writes every mapped body plus the compute/
# SRAM blackbox stubs into build/ppa_closure_top.v.  The compute leaves stay
# blackboxed, so measurement_scope stays PARTIAL_HIERARCHICAL and
# sta_crit_path_ps is a control-plane pre-layout path, never a full-chip Fmax.
#
# Round-62 (harness fix): mla_kv_cache (the only memory-bearing submodule in
# this closure) is now read as a (-lib) blackbox stub that
# scripts/remote_yosys_round.sh appends to the closure partition's
# build/ppa_bb_<name>.v -- the prologue `blackbox m:*mla_kv_cache*` does NOT
# empty a module body in this Yosys build, so without the -lib stub the
# whole-design `memory_map` below expanded its $paramod$ body into ~245,760
# write-mux/$dff cells and the following whole-closure write_verilog dropped
# every control-plane body (sta_body_check 6/6 missing).  With the stub the
# body never enters the closure netlist: its instances stay timing blackboxes
# and the write set is small enough for the six bodies to land.  The 245,760
# bits remain counted under the cp_fsm partition's real body; top_level_sta's
# own memory row is excluded from the aggregate, so no SRAM bit is lost.
# Pass 1: map every non-top module of the closure (the control-plane leaves)
# independently, then the top's glue in pass 2.  Blackboxed modules (the
# compute leaves and mla_kv_cache) are skipped by techmap/abc automatically.
select @PART_TOP@ %n
techmap
clean
log "CLOCK_TARGET_NS=10.0 @PART_TAG@ ABC_D10000 PRE_LAYOUT_ESTIMATE"
# 2026-09-10 fanout repair: `buffer` builds the library buffer tree on the
# mapped netlist before `stime`. See the note right before `design -reset`.
# 2026-09-10 flop-Q PI handover (see the note before `# ===CLOSURE_FLOW_BEGIN===`):
# `dfflibmap` now runs BEFORE the `abc` below, which no longer carries `-dff`, so
# the flops are mapped to the same Liberty's `DFF_X1`/`DFFR_X1` cells first and
# their Q nets reach ABC on the CI/PI side -- the precondition this round's
# `buffer` test needs.
dfflibmap -liberty LIB
abc -liberty LIB -D 10000 -constr build/ppa_measure.constr -script +strash;map;topo;buffer,-p;stime
log "CLOSURE_LEAF_ABC_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
clean
memory_collect
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.closure_leaf_area.stat
stat -liberty LIB
log "CLOSURE_LEAF_AREA_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# Pass 2: map the closure top LAST.  Its control-plane submodule instances are
# already-mapped blackboxes, so this reports/maps the top's own glue only.
select @PART_TOP@
techmap
clean
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.closure_pre_abc.stat
stat
log "CLOSURE_PRE_ABC_STAT_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# 2026-09-10 fanout repair: `buffer` (see the note before `design -reset`).
# 2026-09-10 flop-Q PI handover: `dfflibmap` now runs BEFORE this `abc` (no
# `-dff`).  The pre-ABC `stat` above still shows the unmapped
# `$_DFF_PN0_`/`$_DFF_P_` cells, and the post-`dfflibmap` `stat -liberty` below
# must still show the same `DFFR_X1`/`DFF_X1` counts.
dfflibmap -liberty LIB
abc -liberty LIB -D 10000 -constr build/ppa_measure.constr -script +strash;map;topo;buffer,-p;stime
log "CLOSURE_ABC_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
clean
memory_collect
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.closure_area.stat
stat -liberty LIB
log "CLOSURE_AREA_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# Round-71 (closure export repair): Yosys 0.64 defines `%n` as *selection
# inversion*, not hierarchy descent, and `write_verilog -blackboxes` writes
# only blackbox modules.  The old `select <top> %n; select -add <top>; write
# -selected -blackboxes` therefore produced a 121-byte comment-only closure
# even though the mapped control-plane cells still existed in memory.  Build a
# whole-module transitive closure explicitly with `%s` (modules implementing
# selected cells), then export only those complete real modules.  The STA
# post-processing path appends any missing compute/SRAM timing-blackbox stubs;
# they must not replace the real closure body at this write point.
# Round-73 (harness repair): `read_liberty -lib LIB` is NOT needed here and is
# removed.  The liberty was already loaded by the pass-1/pass-2
# `dfflibmap -liberty LIB` / `abc -liberty LIB` above, and its 135 cell types
# are cell-library entries, not `%s`-reachable submodules -- `select -list-mod`
# confirms the closure still resolves to exactly the 8 real hierarchy modules
# (sta_closure_module_count=8), so this line could never change the closure
# write set.  It was a leftover from the Round-67 "register standard cells"
# hypothesis, which Round-71 already superseded: the body loss was caused by
# the pre-Round-71 `hierarchy -top` pruning, not by unregistered cells.
select -clear
memory_map
select -set closure_modules @PART_TOP@
# A fixed depth is intentional and bounded.  This closure has fewer than 12
# RTL hierarchy levels; each expansion is monotonic, so later passes are
# no-ops once the transitive module set is complete.
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select -set closure_modules @closure_modules %c %s %u
select @closure_modules
log "CLOSURE_WRITE_MODULE_LIST_BEGIN=@PART_TAG@"
select -list-mod
log "CLOSURE_WRITE_MODULE_LIST_END=@PART_TAG@"
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.closure_write_pre.stat stat
write_verilog -selected build/ppa_closure_top.v
log "CLOSURE_NETLIST_WRITTEN=@PART_TAG@"
log "MEASURE_PARTITION=@PART_TAG@"
log "MEASUREMENT_SCOPE=@PART_SCOPE@"
# ===CLOSURE_FLOW_END===
# ===NORMAL_FLOW_BEGIN===
# Round-21 (measurement completion): the global `techmap` that previously
# expanded EVERY module of the partition at once is removed.  Round-08 evidence
# showed the QK^T path (mla_attention_head's 48-term 24-bit signed dot-product
# array) expanding into a whole column of $fa/$alu/$lcu_brent_kung template
# instances inside that shared techmap ("No more expansions possible") before
# any per-module ABC started, so a timeout discarded every module's area.  The
# techmap below now runs per pass, immediately before that pass's own abc
# window: the leaves' small netlists are expanded/mapped first and their area
# emitted, then the top is expanded alone in its own bounded window.  A heavy
# top can no longer flood the leaves' shared netlist or discard their real area.
# Pass 1: map every non-top module (the leaves) independently, then emit their
# area immediately.  The complement %n of the bare top-module pattern selects
# all other modules whole; blackbox modules are excluded automatically, so the
# control-plane closure's blackboxed leaves are not mapped here.
select @PART_TOP@ %n
techmap
clean
# Per-pass pre-ABC cell-count stat on this pass's techmapped netlist: keeps the
# round-04 "heaviest leaf" evidence (e.g. sigmoid_from_q14_mod = 419,612 cells)
# per pass.  It emits "Number of cells" per selected module only -- no "Chip
# area" line and no $mem_v2 memory bits -- so it does not disturb the
# area/memory greps downstream.
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.leaf_pre_abc.stat
stat
log "LEAF_PRE_ABC_STAT_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# Round-47: same 10 ns ABC target as the top/closure passes (see
# CLOCK_TARGET_NS marker above / below).
log "CLOCK_TARGET_NS=10.0 @PART_TAG@ ABC_D10000 PRE_LAYOUT_ESTIMATE"
# 2026-09-10 fanout repair: `buffer` (see the note before `design -reset`).
# 2026-09-10 flop-Q PI handover (see the note before `# ===CLOSURE_FLOW_BEGIN===`):
# `dfflibmap` now runs BEFORE the `abc` below, which no longer carries `-dff`, so
# the flops are mapped to the same Liberty's `DFF_X1`/`DFFR_X1` cells first and
# their Q nets reach ABC on the CI/PI side; the pre-ABC `stat` above still shows
# those unmapped flop cells.
dfflibmap -liberty LIB
abc -liberty LIB -D 10000 -constr build/ppa_measure.constr -script +strash;map;topo;buffer,-p;stime
log "LEAF_ABC_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
clean
memory_collect
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.leaf_area.stat
stat -liberty LIB
log "LEAF_AREA_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# Pass 2: map the partition top LAST in its own bounded window.  Its submodule
# instances are already-mapped blackboxes, so this reports the top's own gates.
# The top's arithmetic is techmapped only now -- this is the window where a
# heavy top (e.g. the QK^T multiplier array) is expanded and mapped, isolated
# from the leaves by the per-pass structure above.
select @PART_TOP@
techmap
clean
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.top_pre_abc.stat
stat
log "TOP_PRE_ABC_STAT_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# 2026-09-10 fanout repair: `buffer` (see the note before `design -reset`).
# 2026-09-10 flop-Q PI handover (same as the leaf pass above): `dfflibmap` now
# runs BEFORE this `abc` (no `-dff`).  The pre-ABC `stat` still shows the
# unmapped flop cells and the post-`dfflibmap` `stat -liberty` below must still
# show the same `DFFR_X1`/`DFF_X1` counts.
dfflibmap -liberty LIB
abc -liberty LIB -D 10000 -constr build/ppa_measure.constr -script +strash;map;topo;buffer,-p;stime
log "TOP_ABC_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
clean
memory_collect
tee -o build/ppa_measure.@PART_TAG@.top_area.stat
stat -liberty LIB
log "TOP_AREA_DONE=@PART_TAG@"
# Round-48 (measurement completion, harness fix): map the collected $mem_v2
# ROMs (the PWL case-table lookups that `memory_bmux2rom` turned into $mem
# during the `memory -nomap` lowering) back into combinational mux logic
# IMMEDIATELY BEFORE the netlist write.  This runs AFTER the top_area stat
# above, so this partition's area/timing/memory-bit accounting is already on
# record and is NOT changed.  Without it, `write_verilog` emits each ROM as a
# behavioral `reg [...] mem [...]` + `initial begin ... end` block, which the
# post-partition OpenSTA/OpenROAD structural reader rejects as STA-0171 (first
# observed on build/ppa_mapped_decay_from_sigmoid_mod.v:22315).  After
# `memory_map` the ROM becomes $mux/$pmux cells that `write_verilog` emits as
# structural `assign` expressions.  `select -clear` first so memory_map covers
# the WHOLE partition (leaves included), not just the stale top-only selection
# that survived pass-2 techmap/abc/clean.
select -clear
memory_map
# Round-59 (harness repair): after `select -clear` (required so `memory_map`
# covers the WHOLE partition) this Yosys build's `write_verilog` follows the
# CURRENT selection -- even WITHOUT `-selected` -- so an emptied selection
# emits no module body (Round-55 evidence, questionAndsolution.md).  Round-58
# tried `write_verilog` without `-selected` on that empty selection and would
# repeat the Round-55 empty write; Round-56's `select *` re-selection was also
# not reliable (Round-57 still raised ORD-2013 "LEF master mem_arbiter4 not
# found" with all six partitions exiting 0 and mem_arbiter4 area measured).
# Re-select the WHOLE partition with the SAME proven pass-1/pass-2 patterns
# (`<top> %n` = every leaf module, then `-add <top>` = the top), then write
# the selected modules plus blackbox stubs.  `-noattr` is dropped so the
# `(* blackbox *)` marker on the `-blackboxes` stubs survives: an empty stub
# without that marker is a missing-LEF leaf (ORD-2013) rather than a linkable
# timing blackbox.  The assembled combined netlist is verified by the
# STA_BODY_CHECK= markers in scripts/remote_yosys_round.sh.
select @PART_TOP@ %n
select -add @PART_TOP@
write_verilog -selected -blackboxes build/ppa_mapped_@PART_TAG@.v
log "MAPPED_NETLIST_WRITTEN=@PART_TAG@"
log "MEASURE_PARTITION=@PART_TAG@"
log "MEASUREMENT_SCOPE=PARTIAL_HIERARCHICAL"
# ===NORMAL_FLOW_END===
